needle='### 2.2.3 Afghanistan and NBCC case materials\n\nThe public connectivity indicators establish that internet use and fixed-broadband subscription levels are limited, but cannot establish the network conditions or software design at NBCC. NBCC’s project portfolio and MIS may provide a useful construction-sector case. The researcher identifies the MIS at mis.nishatbrothers.com as their own project. Before any case-specific claim is included, the researcher will preserve the relevant project-page URL or approved export, record the access date, and distinguish publicly visible project descriptions from private system documentation. No claim about NBCC’s offline operation, database, synchronization, or particular modules is treated as established by this proposal alone.\n'
addition='''
### 2.2.4 Sava.af vendor-described offline architecture

Sava.af presents itself as Afghan accounting and trade-management software covering purchasing, sales, stock, customer credit, expenses, and multiple currencies. Its product pages state that daily work runs on the user’s device, that an encrypted server copy is maintained, and that operations are saved locally and synchronized automatically when connectivity returns. The page also claims that the application can be opened in a mobile browser without installation and that invoice history is retained [11]. The “offline card” graphic illustrates a disconnected device continuing work, queued transactions, and synchronization after reconnection [11].

These are vendor statements and a promotional illustration, not independent measurements. The public pages do not identify the client storage technology, backup cadence, synchronization protocol, retry and idempotency rules, conflict-resolution behavior for simultaneous edits, encryption algorithms or key custody, or test conditions behind the claim that work can continue for a week offline. This proposal therefore records a high-level architecture hypothesis—local operational state, a remote encrypted backup, and later synchronization—but does not attribute unreported implementation details to Sava.af. The case is useful for refining the review’s extraction criteria and for asking what evidence is needed to evaluate an offline-first claim.

**Figure 2. Sava.af’s vendor-published offline card. The image is illustrative product material, not independent validation.**

[[FIG: sava]]
'''
